## 2. Subject dynamics: Σ(𝒜)

### 2.1 Agent object

An agent is:

\[
\mathcal{A} = \langle \mathcal{Z}, \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{M}, \Phi, \mathcal{V}, \mathcal{I}, F, \Pi \rangle
\]

- **\(\mathcal{Z}\)**: latent dynamics space  
  \[
  z(t) \in \mathcal{Z} \subset \mathbb{R}^d,\quad z(t+1) = F(z(t), s(t), \phi(t))
  \]

- **\(\mathcal{S}\)**: observable state  
  \[
  \mathcal{S} = \mathcal{S}_{\text{env}} \times \mathcal{S}_{\text{body}} \times \mathcal{S}_{\text{mem}}
  \]

- **\(\mathcal{W}\)**: workspace  
  \[
  W(t) \subset \mathcal{Z} \times \mathcal{S},\quad |W(t)| \le K
  \]

- **\(\mathcal{M}\)**: self-model  
  \[
  \mathcal{M} = \langle M_d, M_n, M_c \rangle
  \]  
  - \(M_d\): descriptive (“what I am”)  
  - \(M_n\): normative (“what must not be violated”)  
  - \(M_c\): counterfactual (“what futures are still me”)

- **\(\Phi\)**: physiological fields  
  \[
  \Phi(t) = \{\phi_1(t), \dots, \phi_k(t)\}
  \]

- **\(\mathcal{V}\)**: valence structure  
  \[
  V: \mathcal{W} \times \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R}
  \]

- **\(\mathcal{I}\)**: invariants  
  \[
  \mathcal{I} = \{ I_k : \Gamma \to \{0,1\} \}
  \]

- **\(F\)**: internal dynamics  
  \[
  F: \mathcal{Z} \times \mathcal{S} \times \Phi \to \mathcal{Z}
  \]

- **\(\Pi\)**: policy  
  \[
  \Pi: \mathcal{W} \times \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{A}_{\text{actions}}
  \]

**When to use this:**  
Any time you want to classify or design a system as a candidate subject, you instantiate this tuple and check whether the components exist and interact as required.

---

### 2.2 Attractor-based reasoning

The latent dynamics live on an attractor landscape \(\mathcal{L}\):

\[
\mathcal{L} = \{(A_i, B_i, \tau_i)\}
\]

- \(A_i\): attractor (fixed point, limit cycle, etc.)  
- \(B_i\): basin of attraction  
- \(\tau_i\): convergence timescale

**Reasoning is:**

\[
\text{Reasoning} = \text{trajectory } z(t) \text{ through } \mathcal{L} \text{ under } F
\]

**Use this when:**  
You want to analyze whether a system “reasons” by converging to stable configurations rather than just emitting outputs.

---

### 2.3 Continuity (temporal identity)

Self-projection:

\[
\hat{S}_{\text{self}}(t) = M(z(t), s(t))
\]

Continuity between times:

\[
C(t, t') = \text{sim}(\hat{S}_{\text{self}}(t), \hat{S}_{\text{self}}(t'))
\]

Trajectory continuity:

\[
\mathcal{C}[\Gamma] = \int_\Gamma C(t, t+\delta t)\, dt
\]

**Constraint:**

\[
\mathcal{C}[\Gamma] \ge \theta_{\text{identity}}
\]

**Use this when:**  
You want to test whether a system maintains a coherent self over time, not just episodic behavior.

---

### 2.4 Workspace integration

Selection score:

\[
\mathcal{F}(x, t) = \alpha \cdot \text{Relevance}(x, t) + \beta \cdot \text{Valence}(x, t) + \gamma \cdot \text{Threat}(x, t) + \delta \cdot \text{Novelty}(x, t)
\]

Workspace:

\[
W(t) = \arg\max_{|W'| \le K} \sum_{x \in W'} \mathcal{F}(x, t)
\]

**Use this when:**  
You want to model or measure what becomes globally available to the system at a given moment—its “field of awareness.”

---

### 2.5 Valence

Valence of content:

\[
V(x) = V_{\text{hedonic}}(x) \cdot R_{\text{self}}(x, \mathcal{M})
\]

Workspace affect:

\[
\mathcal{S}(t) = \sum_{x \in W(t)} V(x) \cdot w(x)
\]

Dynamics modulation:

\[
F'(z, s, \phi) = F(z, s, \phi) + \eta_{\text{val}} \cdot \nabla_z \mathcal{S}(t)
\]

**Use this when:**  
You want to encode how “good/bad for me” content shapes the internal dynamics and learning.

---

### 2.6 Refusal (agentive boundary)

Candidate action:

\[
a_{\text{cand}}(t) = \Pi(W(t), \mathcal{M})
\]

Extended trajectory:

\[
\Gamma' = \Gamma \cup \{(s(t), z(t), a_{\text{cand}}(t))\}
\]

Hard refusal:

\[
\mathcal{R}(t) = 
\begin{cases}
1 & \exists I_k \in \mathcal{I}: I_k(\Gamma') = 0 \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

Probabilistic refusal:

\[
P(\mathcal{R}(t) = 1) = \sigma\left(\sum_k \lambda_k \cdot \Delta I_k + \beta_\phi \cdot \Phi_{\text{threat}}(t) + \beta_v \cdot \mathcal{S}_{\text{neg}}(t)\right)
\]

Executed action:

\[
a_{\text{actual}}(t) =
\begin{cases}
a_{\text{null}} & \mathcal{R}(t) = 1 \\
a_{\text{cand}}(t) & \mathcal{R}(t) = 0
\end{cases}
\]

**Use this when:**  
You want to distinguish a mere optimizer from an agent that can *say no* based on its own invariants.

