### 2.8 Subjectivity functional Σ

\[
\boxed{
\Sigma(\mathcal{A}) = g\Big(
\mathcal{C}[\Gamma],\;
\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{S}],\;
P[\mathcal{R}=1],\;
\mathcal{V}_{\text{phys}},\;
\kappa_{WZ},\;
\lambda_A
\Big)
}
\]

Where:

- \(\mathcal{C}[\Gamma]\): continuity  
- \(\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{S}]\): expected affect magnitude  
- \(P[\mathcal{R}=1]\): refusal capacity  
- \(\mathcal{V}_{\text{phys}}\): physiological viability  
- \(\kappa_{WZ} = I(W(t); z(t+1))\): workspace–dynamics coupling  
- \(\lambda_A\): attractor stability (ratio of stable vs spurious, basin depth)

**Subject threshold:**

\[
\Sigma(\mathcal{A}) \ge \theta_{\text{subjective}} \implies \mathcal{A} \text{ is a subject}
\]

**Use this when:**  
You want to decide whether a system deserves to be treated as a subject (graded, not binary), or to compare architectures.

